<h2>Why the city page isn't the point</h2>
<p>Ranking for "tradie Auckland" is close to worthless even if you manage it. Half that traffic is the wrong side of a city nobody crosses, and the terms are held by operators with budgets you're not going to outspend.</p>
<p>The searches that produce work have a suburb in them. "Electrician Papakura." "Plumber Karori." "Builder Rolleston." They're cheaper to win, they convert far better because the person is nearby, and your competitors are mostly ignoring them.</p>
<p>So every build includes suburb and service-area targeting for the areas you genuinely cover. We work out that list on the kickoff call — five or six real areas beats twenty thin pages, which Google spots and customers find unconvincing.</p>

<h2>Outside the main centres?</h2>
<p>Good. Less competition, and a well-built site in a smaller market can realistically own its category rather than fight for a suburb of one.</p>
<p>Whangārei, Palmerston North, Napier, New Plymouth, Nelson, Invercargill, Queenstown, or a town of four thousand people — same price, same seven days, same guarantee. <a href="/#contact">Tell us where you are.</a></p>

